  ],
  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "All L1 gold records report measured oral bioavailability values of approximately 68–100% in humans or patients, and none reports a value below the 20% threshold.",
      "card_ids": ["C01","C02","C03","C04","C05","C06","C07","C08","C09","C10","C11","C12","C13","C14","C15","C16"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L3 oral exposure records show measurable oral absorption for structurally related steroidal molecules: canrenone AUC0-24 was 2103 and 4149 ng·hr/ml after 100 mg and 200 mg oral tablets, and ethinylestradiol Cmax values were measurable after oral contraceptive tablets, including products containing drospirenone, the query scaffold.",
      "card_ids": ["C32","C39","C29","C31","C33","C37"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "The L3 records lack IV comparators and do not measure the query molecule's own F, so they are supportive oral-exposure context rather than direct threshold evidence; they provide no affirmative evidence for F < 20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C28","C29","C30","C31","C32","C33","C34","C35","C36","C37","C38","C39"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Context-specific caveats such as hexobarbital 12% in rats, the vildagliptin 50% outlier, and sulbactam poor absorption are species- or molecule-specific and do not transfer to the query or overturn the high direction.",
      "card_ids": ["C23","C26","C27"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "oral_auc_cmax_exposure",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "strengthened",
      "card_ids": ["C28","C29","C30","C31","C32","C33","C34","C35","C36","C37","C38","C39"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct absolute oral bioavailability measurement exists for the query molecule itself.",
    "L3 exposure records lack IV comparators and mostly measure analytes other than the query (ethinylestradiol, canrenone), so F cannot be quantified from them.",
    "No query-matched dose, formulation, prandial state, or species data are available.",
    "Aqueous solubility and first-pass metabolism of the query are not experimentally characterized; the unfavorable solubility prior is not endpoint proof.",
    "Species dependence and literature variability are visible in the source contexts, adding uncertainty to cross-molecule transfer."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Predicted high: all L1 gold records report F of approximately 68–100% in humans, and the new L3 oral exposure records for related steroidal drugs, including oral products containing the query scaffold, are consistent with oral absorption and provide no affirmative F < 20% evidence. Confidence remains moderate because no direct query-specific F measurement or IV comparison is available."
}

